```java
public class ThreadVsProcess {
    static int shared = 0; // lives on the heap — visible to every thread

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Runnable task = () -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) shared++; // both threads touch the same field
        };
        Thread t1 = new Thread(task);
        Thread t2 = new Thread(task);
        t1.start(); t2.start();
        t1.join();  t2.join();
        // Same process, shared heap — no inter-process communication needed
        System.out.println("Shared value: " + shared); // often < 2000 (race!)
    }
}
```

Separate processes could not share 'shared' like this — they would need IPC. The shared heap is exactly why synchronization is required.

```java
public class MainThreadDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // This code runs in the main thread
        System.out.println("Main thread: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());

        // Spawn a non-daemon thread
        var workerThread = new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                Thread.sleep(3000);
                System.out.println("Worker finished after main");
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        });
        workerThread.start(); // Non-daemon by default

        // Spawn a daemon thread
        var daemonThread = new Thread(() -> {
            while (true) {
                System.out.println("Daemon running...");
                try { Thread.sleep(500); } catch (InterruptedException e) { break; }
            }
        });
        daemonThread.setDaemon(true); // Mark as daemon BEFORE start()
        daemonThread.start();

        System.out.println("Main method ending");
        // JVM waits for workerThread but kills daemonThread
    }
}
```

