*For CPU-bound work, a pool near the core count minimizes context switching. I/O-bound work can use a larger pool since threads mostly wait.*

```java
// WRONG for coordination: sleep keeps the lock the whole time
synchronized (lock) {
    Thread.sleep(5000);   // lock is HELD for all 5 seconds
    process();            // no other thread can enter this block meanwhile
}
```

```java
// RIGHT: wait() releases the lock so other threads can make progress
synchronized (lock) {
    while (!ready) {
        lock.wait();      // releases lock and parks until notify()
    }
    process();            // re-acquires the lock automatically before continuing
}
```

sleep is a static timed pause that keeps locks; wait releases the monitor and needs notify() to resume.
